a declined our request to visit  their plants but vice president don  calloway says they only use leftover  wastewood like tree tops and limbs every  piece of fiber that's taken into our  process we can tell you that it's in  fact low value would if we did not  create this service it would be left to  rot on the fourth floor it would be left  to create forest fires foresters say  what we saw at in viva's plants was  something else  so we're here in a cotton field in  southern virginia where we've come to  see one of three in viva plants in the  region  [Music]  our drone captured entire trunks of pine  trees around 100 feet long and stacked  several stories high that could also be  used for paper production so when we  look at those images and i'm sure you've  visited the plants you've seen those  stacks of tens of thousands if not more  tree trunks i'm sorry to interrupt you  jonathan but it's important to not call  them tree trunks we take in tops and we  take in limbs and again to the naked eye  wood looks like wood i've never seen a  limb that tall and certainly in that  area again it can be the tops of a very  very large tree callaway says they never  clear-cut forests however in viva's own  public disclosures show 90 percent of  some harvests including trunks go  straight to them  this wood is then loaded onto conveyor  belts chipped into sawdust and  compressed into pellets literally tons  of these wood pellets are made every  single day but none of them stay here in  the united states we're following this  truck to see where they go  they're trucked around 90 miles to the  port of chesapeake and shipped to europe  where eu regulators consider pellets a  renewable alternative to coal notably  the us does not there's no real doubt  here that wood pellets and frankly woody  biomass are a far superior climate  change mitigation option than fossil  fuels particularly coal  mit researchers concluded wood burns  dirtier than coal and determined it  takes at least 4